{
  "beruf": "lager-logistik",
  "title": "Warehouse and logistics",
  "level": "A2–B1",
  "reviewed": false,
  "_note": "french-for-work run, 2026-11-13: written by hand for France, not yet checked by a second reader. translations.en = English meaning; deHint = German translation (the 'German' tab). Words already in the lexicon are refs, with this job's own example and sense. Working conditions are general orientation only.",
  "intro": "Warehouses and logistics platforms hire many people in France, often through temp agencies (intérim). This deck covers the warehouse and its machines, picking and packing, goods in and dispatch, safety, and team and temp work. Among colleagues, tu is usual; with managers you don't know, use vous.",
  "introEasy": "Do you work in a warehouse in France? Learn the words for your work here.",
  "jobs": [
    "Préparateur de commandes / préparatrice de commandes",
    "Cariste",
    "Magasinier / magasinière",
    "Agent de quai / agente de quai",
    "Opérateur logistique / opératrice logistique"
  ],
  "tipps": [
    "Colleagues say tu and often shout short instructions: « Attention, derrière toi ! », « Tu me passes le transpalette ? » Learn these chunks first, they matter for safety.",
    "Your day follows the scanner: it tells you the emplacement, the quantity and the next order. If it shows an error, say « Ça ne passe pas, il y a une erreur. » and ask your chef d'équipe.",
    "Ask for the safety briefing on your first day: « Où sont les sorties de secours ? » and « Je dois porter quels équipements ? »",
    "Many warehouse jobs start with a mission through an agence d'intérim. Driving a forklift usually needs a CACES and the right training, and safety shoes and a vest are usually required: ask the agency, the employer, France Travail or a union what applies to you. This is general orientation, not legal advice."
  ],
